{title: When I Survey the Wondrous Cross}
{artist: Isaac Watts (1674-1748)}
{key: F}
{copyright: Public Domain}

{sov: Verse 1}
When [F]I survey the [Bb]wondrous [F]cross
On [Dm]which the Prince of [C]glory died,
My [F]richest gain I [Bb]count but [F]loss,
And [Bb]pour contempt on [C]all my [F]pride.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 2}
For[F]bid it, Lord, that [Bb]I should [F]boast,
Save [Dm]in the death of [C]Christ my God!
All [F]the vain things that [Bb]charm me [F]most,
I [Bb]sacrifice them [C]to His [F]blood.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 3}
See, [F]from His head, His [Bb]hands, His [F]feet,
Sor[Dm]row and love flow [C]mingled down!
Did [F]e’er such love and [Bb]sorrow [F]meet,
Or [Bb]thorns compose so [C]rich a [F]crown?
{eov}

{sov: Verse 4}
Were [F]the whole realm of [Bb]nature [F]mine,
That [Dm]were a present [C]far too small;
Love [F]so amazing, [Bb]so di[F]vine,
De[Bb]mands my soul, my [C]life, my [F]all.
{eov}
